                <h2>Why Does Your AI Ad Copy Look Like Everyone Else's?</h2>
<p>The performance marketing team behind a utility app has a recurring headache: AI-generated ad creatives have boosted output efficiency, but CTR has been declining for three consecutive weeks. Upon closer inspection, the copy reads almost identically to competitors—"Unlock efficient new ways," "Make life more convenient."</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. As AI writing tools like Claude, ChatGPT, and Kimi have proliferated in overseas marketing, the homogenization of AI-generated ad copy is eroding team growth efficiency.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: Does AI Copy Homogenization Actually Impact Ad Performance?</h2>
<p><strong>Yes, and more directly than you might think.</strong></p>
<p>Ad systems on Meta, TikTok, and Google track user interaction data. When users repeatedly see structurally similar copy with comparable selling points—even without clicking—the system interprets this as a "low-interest signal" and gradually reduces impression frequency for that creative.</p>
<p>In practical operations, the most direct consequences of homogenized copy include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rising CPM (Cost Per Mille) as the system needs more impressions to find converting users</li>
<li>Declining acquisitions under the same budget</li>
<li>Shortened creative lifespan—what could run for two weeks might die within one</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Verdict</strong>: If AI-generated copy in a specific category consistently underperforms human-written copy by 2%-5% in CTR, homogenization is likely the culprit.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: Why Does AI Always Produce "Universal Clichés"?</h2>
<p>The root cause lies in the <strong>averaging of training data</strong>.</p>
<p>Most AI tools source training data for advertising scenarios from the public internet—vast amounts of mediocre, generic, "human-optimized" copy. This content is "safe" but forgettable. AI hasn't learned "what makes good advertising" but rather "what most ads look like."</p>
<p>Another factor is user prompting habits. Many people use vague instructions like "Write an ad for shoes," prompting AI to output the most common combination of selling points.</p>
<p><strong>Verdict</strong>: The smoother the AI output requires no revisions, the more likely it's producing "greatest common divisor" copy.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: How to Write Prompts That Avoid Clichés?</h2>
<p>The core principle is <strong>setting boundaries for AI, not providing templates</strong>.</p>
<p>❌ Weak prompt example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Help me write a Facebook ad copy for an e-commerce store, highlighting product quality."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>✅ Strong prompt example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Write 5 Facebook ad copies for a $89 outdoor folding chair. Requirements:<br />
1. Angle must focus on "storage pain points in tent camping scenarios"—avoid generic words like "comfort" or "quality"<br />
2. Each copy's opening must differ: numbers, questions, scene descriptions, counter-intuitive assertions<br />
3. Forbidden words: convenient, efficient, premium, effortless<br />
4. Target: 25-35 year-old American backpack travelers, conversational tone"</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Verdict</strong>: The more specific the prompt, the harder it is for AI to "take shortcuts" via generic pathways. Restrictive keywords work better than directional keywords.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: How to Incorporate Brand Differentiation into AI Copy?</h2>
<p>Most AI tools don't know your brand's "persona."</p>
<p>Maintain an internal <strong>Brand Language Guide</strong> (compressed to one page), including:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Brand Tone</td>
<td>Practical, lightly humorous, non-sentimental</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Forbidden Words</td>
<td>Ultimate, revolutionary, pioneering</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fixed Structures</td>
<td>Core selling point first, quantified with numbers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Target User Profile</td>
<td>Early 30s, secondary U.S. cities, fitness-oriented</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Input this guide as background context in AI prompts. One cross-border home goods team measured AI copy similarity dropping from 78% to 41% after implementing the brand guide.</p>
<p><strong>Verdict</strong>: The essence of AI copy differentiation is externalizing and digitizing brand differentiation rules, then feeding them to AI.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: How to Prevent AI from Generating Duplicate Content Across Multiple Accounts?</h2>
<p>A common scenario for overseas teams: one product running simultaneously on Meta, Google, and TikTok, 3-5 ad accounts per platform, 2-3 ad sets per account.</p>
<p>When AI generates in bulk, it's easy to produce "synonym-substituted" duplicates—"Get Started Today" vs. "Start Your Journey Today"—that systems recognize and platforms may flag as low-quality creative stacking.</p>
<p><strong>Practical recommendations</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Build creative fingerprinting</strong>: Use hash values or manual spot-checks to track submitted core phrases</li>
<li><strong>Differentiation triggers</strong>: Pre-set different angles per platform—Facebook for social proof, TikTok for emotional resonance, Google Search for product specifications</li>
<li><strong>Batch management</strong>: Don't let AI generate all creatives at once; generate by batches with intervals, creating varied "context" for each prompt</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Verdict</strong>: Multi-account deduplication is fundamentally about process management, not relying on AI's "voluntary" innovation.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: With Severe AI Copy Homogenization, Should I Switch Tools or Change Strategy?</h2>
<p><strong>Switching tools is the suboptimal choice.</strong></p>
<p>Mainstream AI tools (Claude, ChatGPT, Kimi, ERNIE Bot, etc.) have limited differences in copywriting capability—the core gaps lie in instruction-following accuracy and context window size. Homogenization won't automatically disappear by switching tools.</p>
<p><strong>A more effective approach is restructuring the "human-AI collaboration" division</strong>:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Stage</th>
<th>AI Role</th>
<th>Human Role</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Insight Mining</td>
<td>Generate candidate selling points</td>
<td>Filter 2-3 differentiated ones</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Draft Generation</td>
<td>Batch output multiple versions</td>
<td>Manually tag unique angles</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Revision</td>
<td>Provide rewriting options</td>
<td>Final decision and approval</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Creative QA</td>
<td>Similarity detection</td>
<td>Approve or reject</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>One gaming overseas team implemented this workflow, reducing AI dependency per creative from 85% to 40%, while output efficiency dropped only 15% and average creative lifespan doubled.</p>
<p><strong>Verdict</strong>: AI accelerates文案 production; it doesn't replace creative judgment. Let AI handle "output volume," keep humans responsible for "output quality."</p>
